WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.874
A pension fund puts sixty percent in energy shares and forty

00:03.974 --> 00:08.841
percent in telecom. Energy returns have a standard deviation of twenty-five

00:08.941 --> 00:13.809
percent, telecom fifteen. The expected return is just the weighted average.

00:13.909 --> 00:17.584
Is the risk the weighted average too, twenty-one percent?

00:19.683 --> 00:22.785
Only if the two move in perfect lockstep. The reason

00:22.885 --> 00:25.617
is easiest to see as a picture made of blocks.

00:26.716 --> 00:30.806
Draw each asset's contribution to risk as a length: zero point

00:30.906 --> 00:35.266
six times twenty-five is fifteen for energy, zero point four times

00:35.366 --> 00:39.726
fifteen is six for telecom. Variance is measured in squared units,

00:39.826 --> 00:44.118
so each one contributes a square: two twenty-five and thirty-six.

00:44.216 --> 00:47.717
But the square has two more blocks, the cross terms, one for

00:47.817 --> 00:51.558
each ordering of the pair. Their size is fifteen times six times

00:51.658 --> 00:55.878
the correlation. When rho is one, they fill completely, the whole square

00:55.978 --> 00:59.659
has side twenty-one, and the risk really is twenty-one percent.

00:59.750 --> 01:04.050
At the real correlation, zero point three, the cross blocks shrink

01:04.150 --> 01:08.250
to thirty percent of their size. The variance is three fifteen,

01:08.350 --> 01:12.583
and the risk is seventeen point seven five percent: same expected

01:12.683 --> 01:16.916
return, over three points less risk. That gap is diversification.

01:17.016 --> 01:21.532
Push rho negative and the cross blocks turn red and start subtracting. At

01:21.632 --> 01:25.641
minus one the risk falls to fifteen minus six, just nine percent.

01:25.733 --> 01:28.798
That's the whole theorem for two variables: two squares,

01:28.898 --> 01:31.510
and two cross blocks each carrying a covariance.

01:32.600 --> 01:35.984
Now let the weight vary from all telecom to all energy.

01:36.084 --> 01:39.406
With rho equal to one, risk is a straight line between

01:39.506 --> 01:43.904
fifteen and twenty-five. Lower the correlation and the line sags: every

01:44.004 --> 01:47.389
mix in the middle is safer than the straight line says.

01:47.483 --> 01:51.390
At minus one, the curve touches zero: a mix with three eighths

01:51.490 --> 01:55.332
in energy has no risk at all. Real assets are never perfectly

01:55.432 --> 01:58.563
opposed, but that is exactly the logic of a hedge.

01:59.650 --> 02:03.355
With many assets, the grid gets bigger: n squares on the

02:03.455 --> 02:07.025
diagonal, and n times n minus one cross blocks. Hold n

02:07.125 --> 02:12.394
shares equally, each with thirty percent volatility. If they were uncorrelated,

02:12.494 --> 02:16.268
only the diagonal survives and risk shrinks towards zero.

02:16.366 --> 02:20.597
But real shares share a market. With a correlation of a quarter,

02:20.697 --> 02:25.333
the cross blocks outnumber the squares, and risk levels off at fifteen

02:25.433 --> 02:30.611
percent, however many shares you add. Diversification removes the risk that is

02:30.711 --> 02:34.265
specific to each company, not the risk they all share.

02:35.350 --> 02:39.981
So: for a linear combination, the means carry the weights straight through.

02:40.081 --> 02:44.523
The variance is a grid: squared weights times variances on the diagonal,

02:44.623 --> 02:49.632
and covariance blocks everywhere else. Whenever the correlation is below one, the

02:49.732 --> 02:53.228
mix is less risky than its parts suggest, down to a floor

02:53.328 --> 02:55.625
set by what the assets have in common.
